---
/* Named for the SHAPE, not the component (Slice 109): the owner asked for "a
   pattern for Skeleton", and Skeleton is a component — `bo-skeleton` already
   has its page on /components/state-patterns. What has no page is the SCREEN
   decision around it: whether to show a placeholder at all, what shape it
   takes, and what happens at the moment real content arrives. That is first
   load, and it is a pattern. */
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import Demo from '../../components/Demo.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const matched = `<!-- Placeholders MATCH the shape of what replaces them: three
     columns, the same widths, the same row height. -->
<table class="bo-data-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th scope="col">Order</th><th scope="col">Customer</th>
        <th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Value</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody aria-busy="true">
    <tr>
      <td><span class="bo-skeleton" style="inline-size: 5rem"></span></td>
      <td><span class="bo-skeleton" style="inline-size: 9rem"></span></td>
      <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric"><span class="bo-skeleton" style="inline-size: 4rem"></span></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><span class="bo-skeleton" style="inline-size: 5rem"></span></td>
      <td><span class="bo-skeleton" style="inline-size: 7rem"></span></td>
      <td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric"><span class="bo-skeleton" style="inline-size: 4rem"></span></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>`;

const spinner = `<!-- A REFETCH keeps the rows the user was reading and dims them.
     aria-busy is the programmatic half; the dimming is the visible half. -->
<div class="bo-data-table-container" data-loading="true" tabindex="0">
  <table class="bo-data-table" aria-busy="true">
    <tbody><tr><td>SO-51204</td><td>Halden Marine AS</td></tr></tbody>
  </table>
</div>`;
---
<Gallery title="First load" description="The most-seen state in an ERP and the least-designed: what a screen shows between the click and the data arriving.">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>Who uses it</strong>: everyone, on every screen, before they can do
    anything else. <strong>How often</strong>: several times an hour — it is
    the most-seen state in an ERP and the least-designed.
    <strong>What "done" looks like</strong>: the reader knew what was coming,
    and nothing jumped when it arrived.
    <strong>Not for a REFETCH of data already on screen</strong> — replacing
    rows the user is reading with placeholders throws away their place and
    reads as data loss; dim the existing rows with
    <code>aria-busy</code> instead, as
    <a href={base + '/patterns/list-report'}>list report</a> does. And not for
    waits under about a quarter of a second, where a placeholder flashing in
    and out is worse than nothing at all.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Skeleton, spinner, or nothing</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Three answers, and the wait decides which:</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <caption class="bo-visually-hidden">Which loading treatment to use</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Wait</th><th scope="col">Show</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Under ~250ms</td><td><strong>Nothing</strong></td><td>A placeholder that appears and vanishes reads as a flicker. The screen simply arrives.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>~250ms to ~10s</td><td><strong>Skeleton</strong></td><td>The layout is known, so show it. The reader starts reading structure before content exists.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Over ~10s, or unknown</td><td><strong>Progress, with words</strong></td><td>A skeleton implies "nearly there". Past ten seconds that is a lie — say what is happening and, if you can, how far along.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">A spinner is the fallback when the shape is
    genuinely unknown — a report whose columns depend on the answer. If you
    know the shape, a skeleton beats a spinner, because a spinner says only
    "wait" while a skeleton also says "this is what you will get".</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Shape-matching is the whole technique</h2>
    <Demo code={matched} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Placeholders take the shape of what
    replaces them</strong> — same columns, same widths, same row height. That
    is not decoration: it is what stops the page moving when content lands. A
    generic grey block followed by a three-column table is two layouts, and the
    reader pays for the difference with a jump exactly when they have started
    reading. Vary the widths between rows so it reads as data rather than a
    pattern, and keep the count close to what usually arrives.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Refetch is a different state</h2>
    <Demo code={spinner} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The rows already on screen are still true until
    the new ones arrive, so keep them and dim them. Swapping them for
    placeholders takes away what the reader was in the middle of, which is why
    the opener refuses it. <code>aria-busy</code> carries the programmatic
    half; a table mid-swap is <strong>not</strong> "inactive", so the dimming
    still has to meet contrast — this framework asserts that in
    <code>check:composited</code> rather than assuming it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>The frame that does not move</strong> — the page chrome,
      headings and table header render immediately from what the client already
      knows. Only the unknown parts get placeholders.</li>
      <li><strong>Placeholders</strong> —
      <a href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}><code>.bo-skeleton</code></a>,
      sized to the content they stand in for, inside the region marked
      <code>aria-busy="true"</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>The busy region</strong> — one element carries
      <code>aria-busy</code>, so assistive tech is told once that a region is
      updating rather than once per placeholder.</li>
      <li><strong>The swap</strong> — placeholders are replaced by real nodes
      in one update. Replacing them one at a time makes the layout settle in
      steps, which is the jump this pattern exists to avoid.</li>
      <li><strong>The failure exit</strong> — if the request fails, the
      placeholders must go. A skeleton that never resolves is the worst state
      on this page: it promises content that is not coming.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The server owes the client two things and the
    client owes the reader one.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <caption class="bo-visually-hidden">First-load request and response contract</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Moment</th><th scope="col">What crosses the boundary</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Request sent</td><td>The client renders the frame and the placeholders <em>immediately</em> — it does not wait to be told to.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>200</code></td><td>The full region's markup, swapped into the busy region in one replacement. The response should include enough to remove <code>aria-busy</code> in the same swap.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>4xx</code> / <code>5xx</code></td><td>An error region that REPLACES the placeholders — see <a href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}>error state</a>. htmx 4 swaps non-2xx by default, so this happens with no app code; excluding a status via <code>htmx.config.noSwap</code> is what would leave the skeleton forever.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Timeout</td><td>The client's problem, not the server's: decide a ceiling and fail to the error state at it. "Still loading" after a minute is a bug that looks like patience.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table">
        <caption class="bo-visually-hidden">First-load states</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Treatment</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Loading (first paint)</td><td>Skeleton rows in shape, region <code>aria-busy="true"</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loading (refetch)</td><td>Keep the rows, dim them, <code>aria-busy</code> — never placeholders</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loaded, with data</td><td>One swap, <code>aria-busy</code> removed in the same update</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loaded, empty</td><td>The <a href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}>empty state</a> — and the DIFFERENT one if filters caused it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Failed</td><td>Error state replacing the placeholders, with a retry. The placeholders must not survive this.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reduced motion</td><td>The placeholder stays, the shimmer stops — the block still reserves the space, so nothing is lost but the movement</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Components used</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><span class="bo-badge">complexity 1 of 4</span> —
    one component and a placement rule; the judgement is all in the shape.</p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}>Skeleton</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/state-patterns'}>State</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/data-table'}>Data table</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ['/components/state-patterns', 'Skeleton, empty & error states'],
    ['/patterns/list-report', 'List report'],
    ['/base/motion', 'Motion'],
    ['/getting-started/htmx', 'Using with HTMX'],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
